# The fmt corpus is byte-exact test data, not source.
#
# A fixture's line endings are part of what it tests: `crlf.kp` exists to pin that
# the lexer reads CR as trivia and the formatter emits LF-only output, and the
# pair stops testing anything at all if git normalises its carriage returns away.
# `core.autocrlf=input` does exactly that on commit, which is how the fixture was
# first recorded with zero CR lines in the blob while the working copy had four.
#
# `-text` turns off every end-of-line and text conversion for these paths, so the
# bytes that reach a clone are the bytes the test was written against, whatever
# the cloner's `core.autocrlf` or `core.eol` happens to be.
kelpc/tests/fmt_corpus/** -text
